  <!-- RCI FRAMEWORK -->
  <section id="rci" class="bg-dark">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="shift-head on-dark center">
        <span class="eyebrow on-dark-pill"><span class="dot"></span>THE METHOD</span>
        <h2>The RCI Framework</h2>
        <p><strong style="color:var(--orange-400);">Requirements</strong>, <strong style="color:var(--orange-400);">Context</strong>, <strong style="color:var(--orange-400);">Intent</strong> — the same three questions, reused on anything you build. Here's the structure, the template, and how to read it.</p>
        <p style="font-weight:700;color:#fff;margin-top:12px;font-size:15.5px;">Run it every time you build. That's the whole method.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="rci-flow">
        <div class="rci-node">
          <span class="rci-tag">01 · Requirements</span>
          <h4>What &amp; the constraints</h4>
          <p>What you're building, and the rules it has to follow — audience, length, format, must-includes, must-avoids. 3–5 lines.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rci-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="rci-node">
          <span class="rci-tag">02 · Context</span>
          <h4>The real material</h4>
          <p>Source material, background, reference points — the thing Claude actually works from, not a blank page. In co-work, this is whatever you've loaded into your project's files — docs, notes, spreadsheets, or links.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rci-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="rci-node">
          <span class="rci-tag">03 · Intent</span>
          <h4>What success looks like</h4>
          <p>What the reader feels, does, or understands when it's done. One sentence. If you can't write it, you don't know what you're building yet.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="rci-loop">requirements → context → intent → critique → iterate → repeat</div>

      <div class="demo-panel" style="background:rgba(255,255,255,.03);border:1px solid var(--line-dark);border-radius:22px;padding:40px;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto;">
        <span class="demo-eyebrow" style="color:var(--orange-400);font-weight:600;font-size:12px;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:14px;display:block;">The bare template</span>
        <p style="color:#C4D2D4;font-size:13.5px;margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;">The bare-bones version, with nothing filled in. Copy it exactly as-is, then replace whatever's in brackets with your own build — that's enough to get started.</p>
        <div class="code-block" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <button class="btn-copy" data-copy-target="rci-template">Copy</button>
          <pre id="rci-template">I need to build [X].
Requirements: [constraints].
Context: [source material I'm referencing].
Intent: [what success looks like].
Go.</pre>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div style="max-width:900px;margin:44px auto 0;background:rgba(255,255,255,.03);border:1px solid var(--line-dark);border-radius:16px;padding:24px 26px 6px;">
        <p style="color:#C4D2D4;font-size:13.5px;margin:0 0 18px;line-height:1.6;">A closer look at each section — how long it should run, an example of what it looks like, and the mistake people usually make.</p>
        <table class="rci-table" style="color:#C4D2D4;">
          <thead><tr><th style="color:#9FB4B8;">Section</th><th style="color:#9FB4B8;">How long</th><th style="color:#9FB4B8;">Example</th><th style="color:#9FB4B8;">Common mistake</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody style="color:#C4D2D4;">
            <tr style="border-bottom-color:var(--line-dark);"><td class="rci-r">Requirements</td><td style="color:#C4D2D4;">3–5 lines</td><td style="color:#C4D2D4;">"Audience, length, format, must-includes, must-avoids."</td><td style="color:#C4D2D4;">Describing source material here — that's Context, not Requirements.</td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom-color:var(--line-dark);"><td class="rci-r">Context</td><td style="color:#C4D2D4;">Variable</td><td style="color:#C4D2D4;">"The doc, notes, or data you're actually working from."</td><td style="color:#C4D2D4;">Stuffing Requirements into Context because you're not sure what you need.</td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom-color:var(--line-dark);"><td class="rci-r">Intent</td><td style="color:#C4D2D4;">1 sentence</td><td style="color:#C4D2D4;">"What the reader feels, does, or understands when it's done."</td><td style="color:#C4D2D4;">Skipping it. If you can't write Intent, you don't know what you're building yet.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p style="color:#9FB4B8;font-size:12.5px;padding:16px 0 18px;margin:0;line-height:1.6;">None of these are hard failures — Claude can usually still parse a merged brief. But keeping Requirements and Context separate makes it easier for both you and Claude to know what's actually being asked.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CUSTOM INSTRUCTIONS -->
  <section id="instructions">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="shift-head">
        <span class="eyebrow on-light"><span class="dot"></span>BLOCK 2 · SET UP ONCE</span>
        <h2>Custom Instructions Template</h2>
        <p>These are the rules Claude follows every time you work in this project. Copy the block, change what's in brackets.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="code-block" style="max-width:760px;">
        <button class="btn-copy" data-copy-target="ci-template">Copy</button>
        <pre id="ci-template">You are my collaborator on [PROJECT NAME].

My role: [YOUR ROLE — e.g., "VP of Engineering", "Founder", "Director of Marketing"]
What we're building: [YOUR PROJECT TYPE — e.g., "portfolio site", "board pitch deck", "quarterly dashboard"]
Success looks like: [1 sentence — e.g., "hiring managers understand my work in 30 seconds"]

Tone: direct, peer-level, no fluff. Push back when my brief is vague.
Always ask for the missing context rather than guessing.
Save artifacts to /[PROJECT NAME]/ so I can find them later.
When iterating, surface what you changed and why — don't just hand me a new draft.</pre>
      </div>

      <div style="max-width:760px;margin-top:8px;">
        <div class="why-table">
          <div class="why-row"><span class="wl">My role</span><span class="wr">Calibrates Claude's response register — a founder and a VP get different defaults.</span></div>
          <div class="why-row"><span class="wl">Success looks like</span><span class="wr">This is your Intent in miniature. If you can't write it, you don't know what you're building.</span></div>
          <div class="why-row"><span class="wl">Push back when vague</span><span class="wr">Teaches Claude to refuse bad inputs — critical for the critique-pause skill in Block 5.</span></div>
          <div class="why-row"><span class="wl">Ask, don't guess</span><span class="wr">Prevents Claude from hallucinating when your brief is incomplete.</span></div>
          <div class="why-row"><span class="wl">Surface what changed</span><span class="wr">Makes iteration visible — you see the reasoning, not just a new draft.</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CRITIQUE PAUSE -->
  <section id="critique" class="bg-paper">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="shift-head">
        <span class="eyebrow on-light"><span class="dot"></span>BLOCK 5 · THE HEART OF THE CLASS</span>
        <h2>The Critique Pause</h2>
        <p>Ninety seconds, three critiques, one specific pushback. This is the actual skill you're building today.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="critique-steps">
        <div class="cs-step"><span class="cs-n">01</span><p>Read what Claude gave you. Don't judge yet — just notice what it did and what it missed.</p></div>
        <div class="cs-step"><span class="cs-n">02</span><p>Write down three critiques. What's wrong? What's missing? What's there that shouldn't be?</p></div>
        <div class="cs-step"><span class="cs-n">03</span><p>Pick the strongest one — the one that would change the artifact the most.</p></div>
        <div class="cs-step"><span class="cs-n">04</span><p>Brief Claude on that one specific change. Not "make it better" — a specific pushback.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div style="max-width:700px;">
        <p style="font-size:13.5px;color:var(--slate-500);margin-bottom:14px;">Three patterns for a specific pushback:</p>
        <div class="pattern-list">
          <p class="pattern-item">"You opened with [X], but the audience actually needs [Y]. Rewrite the opener."</p>
          <p class="pattern-item">"You included [X], but that's not relevant to [audience]. Cut it."</p>
          <p class="pattern-item">"You assumed [X], but I actually need [Y]. Adjust."</p>
        </div>
        <p class="reassure" style="margin-top:20px;">Want project-specific critique prompts instead? Each <a href="#fallback" style="color:var(--orange-600);">fallback project</a> comes with three ready to use.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SAVE & ORGANIZE -->
  <section id="organize">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="shift-head">
        <span class="eyebrow on-light"><span class="dot"></span>BLOCK 6 · BEFORE YOU LEAVE</span>
        <h2>Save, Name, Organize</h2>
        <p>Leave with a project you can actually reuse — not a pile of chat history.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="organize-grid">
        <div class="org-card">
          <h3>Folder structure</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><span class="mono">/source</span><span>The material you loaded in</span></li>
            <li><span class="mono">/drafts</span><span>Your iteration history</span></li>
            <li><span class="mono">/final</span><span>Your best artifact</span></li>
